--TEST--
a `Core\Attributes` retrieval reads its target and its written member name syntactically, so a target that is only a `callable` value and a member name naming no declaration are both refused where they are written, and `get` refuses a shape two attached literals satisfy (`rule:attributes/structural-retrieval` and `rule:attributes/retrieval-folds-while-checking`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:attributes/reading-attributes-back-core-attributes-get-and-all
// `rule:attributes/structural-retrieval`: the target is read where it is written and
// never evaluated, and a *written* member name is checked against the target's
// real declarations, because the empty result a misspelling folds to is the
// one a correct retrieval of an absent attribute gives and nothing later can
// tell the two apart. A computed `$member` has no name to check and is the one
// spelling that does fall back to that empty result.
type Tag = {name: string};

#[Tag(name: "class-a")]
#[Tag(name: "class-b")]
class Target {
    #[Tag(name: "prop-a")]
    public int $rows = 0;

    public function run(#[Tag(name: "param-a")] int $id): int {
        return $id;
    }
}

// The accepted half first, so a spelling that stops being accepted fails here
// rather than as a missing refusal. Every target below is written out, every
// member name names a real declaration, and the class's two matching literals
// are read with `all`, which is what `get`'s refusal names as the fix.
array<Tag> $both = Core\Attributes::all<Tag>(Target::constructor(...));
?Tag $property = Core\Attributes::get<Tag>(Target::constructor(...), "rows");
?Tag $parameter = Core\Attributes::get<Tag>(Target::run(...), "id");

// A `callable` variable is a value, and the reference it was built from is not
// written here, so there is nothing at this call to read a declaration out of.
callable $reference = Target::run(...);
?Tag $computedTarget = Core\Attributes::get<Tag>($reference);

// `rows` written one letter wrong. The class declares no `$row`, and folding
// this to the empty result would make the misspelling look like an absent
// attribute for the rest of the program's life.
?Tag $misspelled = Core\Attributes::get<Tag>(Target::constructor(...), "row");

// A parameter name asked of the class rather than of the method that declares
// it. Neither of the class's two rosters names `id`.
?Tag $wrongRoster = Core\Attributes::get<Tag>(Target::constructor(...), "id");

// Two attached literals satisfy `Tag` here, and `get` answers at most one.
?Tag $ambiguous = Core\Attributes::get<Tag>(Target::constructor(...));

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0730]: a `Core\Attributes` retrieval names no declaration
   --> case.nvs:33:50
%A
error[E0798]: a `Core\Attributes` retrieval names `$row`, which `Target` does not declare
   --> case.nvs:38:72
%A
error[E0798]: a `Core\Attributes` retrieval names `$id`, which `Target` does not declare
   --> case.nvs:42:73
%A
error[E0728]: `Target::constructor` has 2 attribute payloads that match this shape
   --> case.nvs:45:19
%A
error: aborting due to 4 errors
